# --- sbt / Scala build output ---
target/
project/target/
project/project/
.bloop/
.bsp/
.metals/
metals.sbt

# --- IDE ---
.idea/
*.iml

# `.vscode/` is mostly per-user state, but `settings.json` and
# `extensions.json` are an environment contract — the editor configuration the
# repository's conventions assume. They travel with the repository; everything
# else in the directory does not.
#
# The pattern must be `.vscode/*`, not `.vscode/`: git does not descend into an
# excluded directory, so a trailing-slash exclusion makes the negations below
# unreachable.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json

# --- Metals worksheets ---

# A `*.worksheet.sc` is a scratchpad: Metals evaluates it on save and prints the
# results as inline decorations. It has to live inside a source directory to
# inherit that build target's classpath, which puts it in the middle of tracked
# sources — but sbt does not compile it (`unmanagedSources` takes `.scala` and
# `.java` only), so nothing in it is covered by the correctness gates.
# Untracked is the honest state for a file the gates do not see.
#
# Put worksheets under `src/test/scala`, never `src/main/scala`. Metals hands
# mdoc the scalacOptions of whichever build target owns the directory, and mdoc
# wraps every statement in a `$doc.binder(...)` call that discards a value. Under
# `Compile`'s `-Werror` that generated line is an error and the worksheet
# evaluates to nothing at all — silently, with the reason visible only in
# `.metals/metals.log`:
#
#     error: No warnings can be incurred under -Werror
#     warning: test.worksheet.sc:12 (mdoc generated code)
#      unused value of type Int
#
# `Test / scalacOptions` carries `-Wall` without `-Werror`, which is the same
# exemption build.sbt already grants exploratory spikes. A worksheet is one.
#
# Delete this rule if a worksheet ever becomes a teaching artifact rather than a
# scratchpad; then it belongs under docs/ with the rest of them.
*.worksheet.sc

# --- Misplaced tool output ---

# Coursier builds its Windows cache path from %LOCALAPPDATA%. A process that
# cannot see that variable resolves it to the literal string "null" and writes
# the cache to ./null in the working directory — tens of megabytes of jars,
# untracked, one careless `git add .` away from the history.
null/

# --- JVM crash / profiling artifacts ---
hs_err_pid*.log
replay_pid*.log
*.hprof
*.jfr

# --- OS noise ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
